#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# distribute

#% text
# `distribute(e)` spreads a call `f(..., a + b, ...)` over the sums among its arguments: the sum of `f` applied to every choice of one term from each sum, the first argument varying slowest. `distribute(e, g)` spreads over the arguments of calls of `g` instead of sums, and `distribute(e, g, f)` only when the head of `e` is `f`.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
distribute(f(a + b, c + d + e))
#% output
#= f(a, c) + f(a, d) + f(a, e) + f(b, c) + f(b, d) + f(b, e)

#% cell
distribute(f(g(a, b), g(c, d, e)), g)
#% output
#= g(f(a, c), f(a, d), f(a, e), f(b, c), f(b, d), f(b, e))

#% cell
distribute(f(a, b), g)
#% output
#= f(a, b)

#% cell
expand((a + b)*(u + v)) === distribute((a + b)*(u + v))
#% output
#= true

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `expand`, `outer`, `tuples`
